</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span><span id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AF1</span> Subgraph Discovery Result</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 From Transformer to AF1 ‣ 3 Methods ‣ All for One: LLMs Solve Mental Math at the Last Token With Information Transferred From Other Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> details the discovery process of the “All-for-One” (AF1) subgraph for the three-operand task <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A+B+C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A+B+C</annotation></semantics></math> via a three-phase ablation and peeking procedure. Here, we present the results for Llama-3-8B.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09650v1/combined_plots_llama3-8b.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/278;" width="476" height="278"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Top: Llama-3-8B performance after making tokens to wait for the first <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{wait}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>wait</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{wait}}</annotation></semantics></math> layers with CAMA, as a function of <math id="S4.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{wait}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>wait</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{wait}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Bottom: for <math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{wait}}=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>wait</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{wait}}=15</annotation></semantics></math> and self-peeking on all non-last tokens, performance of model with last token full-peeking in the next <math id="S4.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{transfer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>transfer</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{transfer}}</annotation></semantics></math> layers and self-peeking afterward, as a function of <math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{transfer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>transfer</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{transfer}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 AF1 Subgraph Discovery Result ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ All for One: LLMs Solve Mental Math at the Last Token With Information Transferred From Other Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (top) tracks the faithfulness when the first <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{wait}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>wait</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{wait}}</annotation></semantics></math> layers of the full transformer are replaced with Context-Aware Mean Ablation (CAMA). Faithfulness remains high for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{wait}}\leq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>wait</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{wait}}\leq 15</annotation></semantics></math> and then collapses sharply at <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{wait}}=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>wait</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{wait}}=16</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that no cross-token interaction before layer index 14 is necessary, but information transfer at layer index 15 is critical (recall that layer index is 0-based).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We then investigate the necessity of information transfer at layer 15 and onward, replacing the attention components with full-peeking using ABP. In particular, we prohibit all non-last tokens to attend to any other token except for itself and the BOS token, while allowing the last token to still attend to every previous token. This operation drastically changes the amount of information flow across tokens. However, we observe no performance degradation, suggesting that non-last tokens do not need to receive information from elsewhere and only need to send information to the last one.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we incrementally remove the last token full-peeking and restrict it to self-peeking (where the last token attends only to itself and the BOS token) from the back. Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 AF1 Subgraph Discovery Result ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ All for One: LLMs Solve Mental Math at the Last Token With Information Transferred From Other Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (bottom) tracks the faithfulness when we modify layer <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{transfer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>transfer</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{transfer}}</annotation></semantics></math> to from 0 to 17 (both inclusive) with the layers <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\geq L_{\mathrm{transfer}}+L_{\mathrm{wait}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>transfer</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>wait</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\geq L_{\mathrm{transfer}}+L_{\mathrm{wait}}</annotation></semantics></math> being self-peeking. We find that performance stays high as long as <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{transfer}}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>transfer</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{transfer}}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, or in other words, the last token can access other tokens at least layer 15 and 16.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, we have identified a subgraph for Llama-3-8B on the task of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A+B+C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A+B+C</annotation></semantics></math> that retains almost full performance, with 14 CAMA layers and 2 information transfer layers followed by last token self-computation. We call this <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AF1<sub id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub">llama</sub></span>. In subsequent sections, we study its performance on other tasks for both Llama-3-8B and Llama-3.1-8B, as well as analogous subgraphs in Pythia and GPT-J.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span><span id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AF1<sub id="S4.SS3.2.1" class="ltx_sub">llama</sub></span> Subgraph Performance</h3>

<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
